WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.809 --> 00:00:13.459
It takes a long time to make a human.

00:00:13.459 --> 00:00:19.340
We can sometimes, I think, get caught up in
our own perceptions of how life works.

00:00:19.340 --> 00:00:25.630
But there are some organisms that don’t
have the luxury of years and years of childhood.

00:00:25.630 --> 00:00:31.390
No, for them, life is brief and they’ve
got to get on with living...and so sometimes

00:00:31.390 --> 00:00:37.110
like with the gastrotrich, they’ll just
go ahead and have their first baby on the

00:00:37.110 --> 00:00:40.730
day they’re born.

00:00:40.730 --> 00:00:44.210
Now we’ve never had the chance to name a
species of microbe.

00:00:44.210 --> 00:00:47.719
We name individuals all the time, this one’s
Neil.

00:00:47.719 --> 00:00:51.909
But naming a species seems like both a thrilling
and weighty task.

00:00:51.909 --> 00:00:58.330
You take this strange, new, tiny thing, and
you assign it an identity that it will never

00:00:58.330 --> 00:01:04.259
know but that other human observers will come
to rely on, ostensibly forever.

00:01:04.259 --> 00:01:09.229
And through the years, people who have faced
this challenge have turned to a wealth of

00:01:09.229 --> 00:01:11.650
imagery and language to guide them.

00:01:11.650 --> 00:01:16.810
There’s the multi-headed serpent of Greek
and Roman mythology, now also the name for

00:01:16.810 --> 00:01:20.200
the multi-cellular, regenerating wonder of
Hydras.

00:01:20.200 --> 00:01:26.799
Or you could go cuter, like the tardigrade,
which translates roughly to “slow stepper”

00:01:26.799 --> 00:01:30.170
which also has the advantage of being accurate.

00:01:30.170 --> 00:01:38.130
And then, you have Gastrotrichs, also known
by their phylum name Gastrotricha.

00:01:38.130 --> 00:01:42.679
These organisms were not named in so flattering
a fashion.

00:01:42.680 --> 00:01:47.620
No, Gastrostricha translates to “hairy stomachs.”

00:01:47.620 --> 00:01:52.920
And when you don’t feel like saying “gastrotrich,”
you can also call them by their common name:

00:01:52.929 --> 00:01:54.219
“hairy backs.”

00:01:54.220 --> 00:02:01.400
But a rose is a rose by any other name, and,
truth is, they do have hairy stomachs.

00:02:01.400 --> 00:02:02.720
And backs.

00:02:02.720 --> 00:02:06.090
All, as we will see, for good reason.

00:02:06.090 --> 00:02:11.720
But if their name doesn’t capture the mythological
glory that other organisms get, there are

00:02:11.720 --> 00:02:18.040
moments, when the light hits just right and
bounces off their little scales, that Gastrotrichs

00:02:18.040 --> 00:02:27.569
resemble iterations of one of the most pervasive
mythological figures of all time: the dragon.

00:02:27.569 --> 00:02:29.390
We should scale back a bit though.

00:02:29.390 --> 00:02:35.319
After all, gastrotrichs are among some of
the smallest metazoans, or we could say, less

00:02:35.320 --> 00:02:37.600
esoterically...animals.

00:02:37.600 --> 00:02:43.880
They range from around 60-500 micrometers
long, though in some very exceptional cases,

00:02:43.890 --> 00:02:47.280
they might get up to several millimeters in
length.

00:02:47.280 --> 00:02:54.230
There are two orders in the Gastrotricha phylum:
Chaetonida, which are mostly found in freshwater,

00:02:54.230 --> 00:02:59.670
and Macrodasyida, which are found in marine
systems, though there are, weirdly, a few

00:02:59.670 --> 00:03:01.160
freshwater species.

00:03:01.160 --> 00:03:07.349
No matter what order they belong to, they
make up part of what’s called the meiofauna.

00:03:07.349 --> 00:03:09.940
These are species grouped by their size.

00:03:09.940 --> 00:03:18.090
Too big to be microfauna, too small to be
macrofauna, the meiofaunal organisms are between

00:03:18.090 --> 00:03:25.650
60 micrometers to 2 millimeters—and they
play a deeply important ecological role, a

00:03:25.650 --> 00:03:33.940
bridge between the micro and the macro, consuming
the former and being consumed by the latter.

00:03:33.940 --> 00:03:40.189
They are a part of this system that brings
nutrients out from the microscopic world,

00:03:40.189 --> 00:03:44.820
into the parts of ecosystems that we can directly
observe.

00:03:52.520 --> 00:03:58.460
Gastrotrichs are among the most abundant species
present in the meiofauna.

00:03:58.469 --> 00:04:04.680
In some habitats, it’s been estimated that
there are around 100,000 individual gastrotrichs

00:04:04.680 --> 00:04:06.489
per square meter.

00:04:06.489 --> 00:04:12.299
And there are around 860 known species counted
across both orders.

00:04:12.299 --> 00:04:19.500
Now, that’s a lot of species of Gastrotrichs,
but it’s actually surprisingly low compared

00:04:19.500 --> 00:04:23.470
to the diversity of other meiofaunal organisms.

00:04:23.470 --> 00:04:30.710
There are, for example, over 2,000 rotifer
species, and approximately 40,000 species

00:04:30.710 --> 00:04:32.630
of nematode.

00:04:32.630 --> 00:04:37.320
Now this doesn’t mean anything bad for gastrotrichs—though it might be why they have yet to be found

00:04:37.320 --> 00:04:39.360
in any extreme kind of habitat.

00:04:39.360 --> 00:04:47.430
But it is remarkable that for microscopic
metazoans, 860 species is low when you put

00:04:47.430 --> 00:04:50.110
it up against some other phyla.

00:04:50.110 --> 00:04:56.050
Returning to their unfortunate but well-earned
name: the stomachs of gastrotrichs are covered

00:04:56.050 --> 00:05:03.310
in hair-like cilia, which they use to glide
along surfaces as they dine on bacteria, algae,

00:05:03.310 --> 00:05:05.810
and other small particles.

00:05:05.810 --> 00:05:11.190
Unfortunately for us, it is hard to observe
and film these hairy stomachs because, well,

00:05:11.190 --> 00:05:13.760
they swim with their bellies down

00:05:13.760 --> 00:05:18.840
We’ve found that when we record gastrotrichs,
we have to be extra careful.

00:05:18.840 --> 00:05:25.690
Their head is covered in cilia and mechanoreceptors
that seem to respond to any slight movement

00:05:25.690 --> 00:05:26.919
in the water.

00:05:26.919 --> 00:05:33.340
So it’s important not to create any accidental
vibrations in the microscope that might startle them.

00:05:33.340 --> 00:05:41.380
Gastrotrich bodies are enclosed in a cuticle
and covered in spines or scales, or sometimes both.

00:05:41.380 --> 00:05:47.280
And at the end of their bodies, many species
have a pair of feet, which hold adhesive tubes

00:05:47.280 --> 00:05:52.729
that they can use to attach to grains of sand
or other objects.

00:05:52.729 --> 00:06:00.870
And underneath the hairy, scaly cuticle, we
see the wonder of a multicellular body: a

00:06:00.870 --> 00:06:07.539
brain, nervous system, simple gut, intestines,
muscles, and more.

00:06:07.539 --> 00:06:14.590
Inside of this chaetonid gastrotrich is an
egg, and when that egg hatches, out will come

00:06:14.590 --> 00:06:22.460
a new gastrotrich, fully developed with all
of those organs in place, and about 2/3 the

00:06:22.460 --> 00:06:25.240
length of a full adult.

00:06:25.240 --> 00:06:32.220
The fact that it hatches so formed is remarkable
on its own, but the whole reproductive cycle

00:06:32.220 --> 00:06:35.860
of chaetonids is a marvel

00:06:35.860 --> 00:06:40.740
Gastrotrichs are not particularly long-living
creatures.

00:06:40.750 --> 00:06:46.000
Their life span is on the order of days to
weeks, which means they have to cram a lot

00:06:46.000 --> 00:06:48.750
of living into a short amount of time.

00:06:48.750 --> 00:06:56.650
When they hatch, chaetonids seem to immediately
begin developing their eggs through parthenogenesis,

00:06:56.650 --> 00:07:02.330
a kind of cloning through which an unfertilized
egg is able to develop into an embryo that

00:07:02.330 --> 00:07:05.460
is genetically the same as the parent.

00:07:05.460 --> 00:07:10.770
In the species and conditions that chaetonid
reproduction has been most extensively documented

00:07:10.770 --> 00:07:17.370
in, you might see a gastrotrich lay an egg
within a day after they themselves hatch.

00:07:17.370 --> 00:07:24.720
And typically, they’ll lay 4 eggs within
4 days that can develop and hatch within a

00:07:24.720 --> 00:07:30.090
day of being laid, producing genetically identical
offspring.

00:07:30.090 --> 00:07:37.050
And those children are then out there themselves
laying eggs, meaning a gastrotrich can become

00:07:37.050 --> 00:07:41.759
a grandmother just a few days after it was
born!

00:07:41.759 --> 00:07:48.150
And here’s another weird thing...sometimes,
they lay a special kind of egg called an opsiblastic

00:07:48.150 --> 00:07:54.470
egg, which has spines and is thicker and larger
compared to the other eggs, qualities that

00:07:54.470 --> 00:07:57.949
keep it protected under harsher conditions.

00:07:57.949 --> 00:08:03.740
Because of this rapid egg laying, we used
to think that chaetonids were entirely made

00:08:03.740 --> 00:08:06.680
up of parthenogenetic females.

00:08:06.680 --> 00:08:12.520
But later observations have revealed that
once these early eggs get laid, the chaetonids

00:08:12.520 --> 00:08:17.580
become hermaphroditic, developing sperm and
eggs.

00:08:17.580 --> 00:08:21.560
What they use the sperm for though is a mystery.

00:08:21.560 --> 00:08:28.939
We just don’t have the observations we need
to understand how chaetonids make use of their

00:08:28.939 --> 00:08:31.840
hermaphroditic phase.

00:08:31.840 --> 00:08:35.539
This life history though characterizes just
the chaetonids.

00:08:35.539 --> 00:08:39.880
Macrodasyoid gastrotrichs seem to all be hermaphroditic

00:08:39.880 --> 00:08:43.630
except for one species, and so their process

00:08:43.630 --> 00:08:44.970
is of course different.

00:08:44.970 --> 00:08:51.320
And yet the result is the same: the direct
development of young gastrotrichs that contain

00:08:51.320 --> 00:08:58.980
the full multicellular plan in their own body,
ready to create the next generation at birth.

00:08:58.980 --> 00:09:06.140
It’s hard to know sometimes what to be most impressed by when you’re looking into the microcosmos.

00:09:06.140 --> 00:09:11.610
Is it the giant unicellular organism, or the
tiny multicellular one?

00:09:11.610 --> 00:09:18.290
Is it giving birth on the day you were born,
or the 40,000 species of nematodes..of course,

00:09:18.290 --> 00:09:25.850
chances are, just minutes ago you had no idea
that the earth was covered in these tiny beasts,

00:09:25.850 --> 00:09:31.810
bridging the words of the micro and macro,
and now we can’t even decide what the coolest

00:09:31.810 --> 00:09:34.410
thing about them is.

00:09:34.410 --> 00:09:40.529
Complexity is its own complex thing, so carry
on, our hairy-bellied friend.

00:09:40.529 --> 00:09:47.670
And thank you for coming on this journey with
us as we explore the unseen world that surrounds us.

00:09:47.670 --> 00:09:54.420
And thank you especially to all of the people
on the screen right now who support us via Patreon.

00:09:54.420 --> 00:09:58.260
These people allow us to continue diving into
these mysteries.

00:09:58.260 --> 00:10:03.980
It’s a joy to do and we’re so happy that
you are compelled to help us out.

00:10:03.980 --> 00:10:08.000
If you want to see more from our Master of
Microscopes, James, you can check him out

00:10:08.000 --> 00:10:09.990
on Jam and Germs.

00:10:09.990 --> 00:10:14.170
And if you want to see more from us, here
at Journey to the Microcosmos, you can find

00:10:14.170 --> 00:10:20.290
us on twitter at twitter.com/journeytomicro
and you can also find us here on youtube at

00:10:20.290 --> 00:10:23.760
youtube.com/microcosmos.

